ndia, she was inspired to found the Missionaries of Charity order. The order began four years later. It has since opened more than 130 houses worldwide to provide comfort and care for the needy, sick and "poorest of the poor." Mother Teresa won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979 for her work with Calcutta's poor and ill. She continued this work even after she herself became sick. "The poor give us much more than we give them," Mother Teresa said in 1977. "They're such strong people, living day to day with no food. And they never curse, never complain." She died on Sept. 5, 1997, at age 87. ## Mother Teresa Had Some Critics At the time, her Missionaries of Charity order had nearly 4,000 nuns. It ran about 600 orphanages, soup kitchens, homeless shelters and medical clinics around the world. While Mother Teresa is known and admired by many around the world, she was not beloved by all. She was criticized for the sometimes bad quality of care in her clinics. She was also criticized for taking donations from a Haitian dictator and an American banker who spent time in jail for financial crimes. Pope Francis was an admirer. He is dedicated to caring for the poor just as Mother Teresa did. During his September 2014 visit to Albania, Francis said he was not only impressed by her courage and toughness, but in some ways he feared it. ## "The Best Christmas Gift" As if to underscore his connection to Mother Teresa, Francis on Friday celebrated Mass at a Caritas charity soup kitchen in Rome. There, during the Catholic religious ceremony, he told homeless people and volunteers that their way is the path to heaven, not that of the wealthy or powerful. Normally, it takes five years to start on the road to sainthood. Pope John Paul II, one of Mother Teresa's greatest champions, began the process a year after she died, convinced of her saintliness. Thomas D'Souza is the Archbishop of Kolkata, the city's Catholic leader. He said the news of the Catholic Church's decision about Mother Teresa was "the best Christmas gift one can get." "We are grateful to God and we are extremely happy," he said. <|eot_id|>
